If $matbold(A)$ preserves dot products, then it preserves norms by taking $bold(x) = bold(y)$. Conversely, if $matbold(A) in O(n)$, the polarisation identity applied to $matbold(A) bold(x)$ and $matbold(A) bold(y)$ gives
$
  2 (matbold(A) bold(x)) dot (matbold(A) bold(y)) = 2 bold(x) dot bold(y).
$
Thus the two conditions are equivalent.
